          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Lorck\",\"addressee\":\"Constance\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\\nJeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly presents Lorck's love for Constance as a true emotional state. His immediate insistence that it is serious reinforces the avowal, regardless of its sincerity or later consequences.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The focal words themselves do not undertake an obligation, promise conduct, or establish a defined relational commitment. Lorck's adjacent offer of himself, his life, and his soul is a separate part of the longer turn and must not be transferred onto the marked love avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Although Lorck speaks under intense affect, the focal expression is a deliberate, intelligible avowal used to persuade Constance after demanding an answer. The evidence does not present these particular words as a reflex-like discharge beyond their assertive emotional content.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg spør, om De, viser mig bort?\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The core force of the focal expression is adequately represented as an avowal of a loving state. Its persuasive role in the confrontation does not establish a distinct residual force requiring O.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lorck addresses Constance during an unwanted declaration of passion. After she rejects his advances and asks what his conduct means, he presses her for an answer, stands before her in a threatening manner, and then avows that he loves her. The avowal is immediately followed by a separate offer of himself, his life, and his soul. Constance rejects the overture and retreats.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression itself contains no undertaking. In the neighbouring sentence, Lorck separately offers Constance himself, his life, and his soul and asks whether she will accept. The exact practical terms are unspecified, and Constance explicitly rejects the offer with “Nej, uf nej” and moves away.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied evidence explicitly identifies the work as Amalie Skram's Constance Ring.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence strongly supports that Lorck experiences and represents intense passion, but it does not make the precise nature, durability, or moral quality of that affection equivalent to a commitment. Later conduct may qualify the reader's view of him, but it does not erase the original avowal's truth-presenting force.\"}"
